## leaf wait --help
Usage: leaf wait [OPTIONS] PAGE

  Watch every page this session holds \u2014 plus PAGE, claimed first, when given.

  Print one page's complete ordered batch, thread context, and response
  requirements as an immutable delivery, whose `acknowledge` says how to confirm
  it. `leaf delivery read <id>` reads that same delivery. Where the host's hook
  carries input into the turn, as in Claude Code, print one line naming the page
  with new input instead, and end.

Options:
  --ack DELIVERY_ID  Confirm receipt of this complete delivery before waiting.
  --help             Show this message and exit.

## leaf delivery read --help
Usage: leaf delivery read [OPTIONS] DELIVERY_ID

  Print DELIVERY_ID with its complete batches and response requirements.

Options:
  --help  Show this message and exit.

## leaf page state --help
Usage: leaf page state [OPTIONS] PAGE

  Fold the log onto the active revision and print the result as one JSON object:
  the active revision's file, standing state, reports, open Asks, thread
  summaries, versions, presence, and bound data. Read the active revision's HTML
  beside it for the document.

Options:
  --help  Show this message and exit.

## leaf thread read --help
Usage: leaf thread read [OPTIONS] PAGE THREAD

  Print one current thread and a page of its exact event history.

  THREAD is any message in the thread.

Options:
  --after SEQ            [default: 0; x>=0]
  --limit INTEGER RANGE  [default: 50; 1<=x<=100]
  --help                 Show this message and exit.

## leaf thread summarize --help
Usage: leaf thread summarize [OPTIONS] PAGE

  Replace --from through --through in the panel with a Markdown summary.

  Both name messages in one thread, which is the thread summarized. The original
  messages remain in the append-only transcript and can always be revealed. A
  later overlapping summary replaces this one.

Options:
  --from MESSAGE     [required]
  --through MESSAGE  [required]
  --text TEXT        summary Markdown (default: stdin)
  --json             print the summary event
  --help             Show this message and exit.

## leaf thread title --help
Usage: leaf thread title [OPTIONS] PAGE THREAD

  Name THREAD, by any message in it, for the thread panel; repeat to rename it.

  Choose a few descriptive words when first handling a thread. Keep the title
  stable unless its subject changes. This adds no message or reply.

Options:
  --text TEXT  short descriptive title (at most 80 characters)  [required]
  --json       print the title event
  --help       Show this message and exit.

## leaf status --help
Usage: leaf status [OPTIONS] PAGE {working|waiting|idle} [DETAIL]

  Set the agent's banner state.

  Use working with DETAIL, required, naming your current work and its subject,
  or waiting with the answer you want from the user. Waiting without DETAIL
  invites text comments. Use idle when finished; unacknowledged input and
  unanswered user moves prevent it.

  With working, --on names an open thread, by any message in it, or a widget:
  whatever a delivered event gives as its address, which then reads Working. The
  user sees DETAIL beside that subject as well as in the banner. Your next reply
  ends a thread claim; `leaf page stamp --completes` ends a widget claim. Renew
  the status as work changes: a claim left after your turn ends, or without
  updates, eventually reads as stalled.

Options:
  --on SUBJECT  The open thread or widget this work is about.
  --help        Show this message and exit.

## leaf thread open --help
Usage: leaf thread open [OPTIONS] PAGE

  Open a thread as the agent (--text or stdin): anchored where --quote or
  --section points at a passage, general where neither does \u2014 a question about
  the work as a whole.

  The user answers it in the browser. Refuses a quote the active revision does
  not hold, or holds more than once.

Options:
  --quote TEXT   passage text from the active revision
  --section ID   element ID to anchor or scope --quote
  --part ID      declared visual part within --section
  --text TEXT    comment text (default: stdin)
  --markup TEXT  widget markup to render after the text, validated here
  --json         print the comment event instead
  --help         Show this message and exit.

## leaf thread reply --help
Usage: leaf thread reply [OPTIONS] PAGE [THREAD]

  Post a threaded reply as the agent (--text or stdin).

  Answer user input with --for EVENT_ID. With exactly one outstanding reply in
  this turn's opened delivery, omit it to select that reply. THREAD, by any
  message in it, posts a new agent message there instead, refused while that
  thread owes a reply.

  --quote, --section, and --part move the thread's current anchor; --detach
  removes it when the subject leaves the page. The original anchor stays in the
  log. A reply validates and activates any changed source before posting.

Options:
  --for EVENT_ID  delivery event whose current reply obligation this answers
  --quote TEXT    new passage text to move this thread onto
  --section ID    new element ID, or scope for --quote
  --part ID       new declared visual part within --section
  --detach        remove the current page target when its subject leaves the
                  page
  --text TEXT     reply text (default: stdin)
  --markup TEXT   widget markup to render after the text, validated here
  --awaits        the reply's prose asks the user a question
  --json          print the reply event instead
  --help          Show this message and exit.

## leaf thread edit --help
Usage: leaf thread edit [OPTIONS] PAGE MESSAGE

  Replace the visible text of MESSAGE, an agent-authored comment or reply.

  The original and every revision remain in the append-only event log. Frozen
  widget markup is not editable.

Options:
  --text TEXT  replacement text (default: stdin)
  --json       print the edit event instead
  --help       Show this message and exit.

## leaf thread resolve --help
Usage: leaf thread resolve [OPTIONS] PAGE THREAD

  Close THREAD, by any message in it, as the agent.

  The user ordinarily closes a thread; the Leaf skill's references/threads.md
  says when the agent does. Refuses a thread that asked for a version until a
  stamped version answers it. The panel names who resolved it.

Options:
  --json  print the resolve event instead
  --help  Show this message and exit.

## leaf page report --help
Usage: leaf page report [OPTIONS] PAGE WIDGET VERB [NAME=VALUE]...

  Report a state change onto a page widget, as a worker.

  The verb and its fields are the widget's own agent-written x-state verb \u2014
  `leaf page report <page> t-parser status status=review` moves a task. The page
  paints the report live as provisional news; it stands until a version absorbs
  or overrules it, and the page's watcher wakes to fold it in.

Options:
  --json  print the report event instead
  --help  Show this message and exit.

## leaf page check --help
Usage: leaf page check [OPTIONS] PAGE

  Check PAGE/index.html.

  Runs deterministic markup checks. --render also checks the drawn page in the
  host's browser: whichever executable LEAF_BROWSER_EXECUTABLE, CHROME_PATH, or
  CHROME_BIN names, else the installed Chrome, else the first browser on PATH.

Options:
  --render  also check the rendered page in the host's browser
  --help    Show this message and exit.

## leaf page stamp --help
Usage: leaf page stamp [OPTIONS] PAGE

  Stamp PAGE/index.html with a changelog.

  Checks the exact source first, then records it as the next public version.
  Repeat --completes for each active widget work claim this version completes. A
  widget claim otherwise survives unrelated versions, and a version cannot
  silently remove its page target.

Options:
  --text TEXT         changelog text (default: stdin)
  --completes WIDGET  active widget work this version completes (repeatable)
  --json              print the note event instead
  --help              Show this message and exit.
